Date: Tue, 4 Jan 94 15:55:57 EST
From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: The Gene Project

In message , John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us> says:
>The DNA in one cell contains enough information to reconstruct
>you just as you were on the day you were born,[....]

I doubt this.  I have minored in biology and had DNA sequencing training, 
so I know the theory, but I still doubt this.  I have an identical twin 
brother.  I have studied many sets of identical twins.  Identical twins 
should have the identical DNA, but they are not born identically.  They are 
born with different weights, heights, structural differences, different 
appearences, and even different personalities.  A clone of my DNA or my 
brother's DNA would produce a person as different from each of us as he and 
I are different.  We would be no more than similar-looking brothers.

A copy of my DNA on file, or a clone of me running around would make me 
feel no more immortal than if my brother sent me a message saying, "Ha, Ha, 
I'm immortal and you're not!"  His survival has no part in my future goals 
in any way.
