Date: Tue, 4 Jan 94 21:04:05 EST
From: Andy Wilson <ajw@Think.COM>
Subject: No More Prisons

   Date: Mon, 3 Jan 1994 19:17:53 CST  
   From: "Kathleen Cadmus" <kcadmus@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>

   Several recent posters about imprisonment have strayed far from what I
   conclude to be the only view consistent with Extropian principles.
   Imprisonment should be totally abolished!

   There seem to be four justifications for imprisonment:

       1) Isolation
       2) Rehabilitation
       3) Restitution
       4) Retribution

   I now proceed (IMHO :-)) to shoot them all down.

I only disagree with your argument against Isolation, but pretty much
agree with what you say on the others...

   1) Isolation -

   I will not address the gross impropriety of our existing legal codes. Let's
   keep this discussion theoretically sound by assuming a minimal set of legal
   proscriptions against the usual crimes against self and property including
   breach of contract. From my own perspective, everyone has some non-zero
   probability of future crimes against me. Should I imprison you all to keep
   myself safe from you? Maybe only those with such a probability beyond a
   given threshold level? (Is this the justification for locking up a drunk
   driver despite the lack of demonstrable harm done?) We are on the
   slipperiest of slopes here. Is the crime rate higher than average in the
   Black community? Lock them all up?

In the distinction between demonstrated violent behavior and the lack
of it I see no slope to slip on.  That is my criterion.

   Sorry but we all need to accept the responsibility of protecting our selves
   and our possessions *without* resorting to the coercive acts which we claim
   to deplore. What is more anti-extropian than placing another human being in
   a cage? Protection from possible future criminal acts is a feeble excuse for
   this scaredy-cat statist behavior.

I disagree with one of your premises,  the deploring of all coercive acts.

I don't claim to deplore all coercive acts.  Coercive acts made in
self-defense, or the defense of others or property, are justified.
I have committed such acts in the past and will in the future when
necessary.

It is clear to me that it is possible for a person to demonstrate behavior
which shows that they do not value the "rights" that most of us agree on,
for example the right not to be killed.  In that case, it is justified
to isolate them to prevent further antisocial acts.  I am open to other
methods of preventing criminal behavior,  but isolation is a pretty safe
way to prevent further murders or rapes by the same individual.

In the case where a person has not demonstrated violent behavior or threats
thereof,  then of course there is no justification for coercive acts made 
in self-defense.

Andy




