Subject: LAW:  The Law by Frederic Bastiat

From: "Harry S. Hawk"

"What leaves me the most chilled is that for 144 years such strong
views on liberty have existed and yet socialism has in my mind only
got stronger. True we have had the "collapse of communism" and that is
to our credit. Still socialism is alive and well in every western-like
country except perhaps Mexico and Chile."
..........................................

When I think of all of the books sitting in libraries which have been 
written by great thinkers on the subject of freedom, liberty, human 
dignity, etc.,  I wonder why so many people are so ignorant of these 
important ideas.

Obviously, they have not read the books.

That's rather dependent though, to think that if we did not read these 
works then we would not be able to know the difference between justice 
and injustice.  Yet even though many lawyers study heavy tomes of 
historical data which should give them some insight into the proper 
human condition, they have the reputation for easily overlooking simple 
truths, showing an amazing disregard for basic morality.

I have wondered why it would be easy for someone to, as Bastiat said, 
pervert the concept of Law such that it would become a weapon of the 
State against the citizenry, instead of their protection against crime; 
 I don't think it has to do with socialism  -   socialism does not 
exist of its own power, as it does not maintain itself of its own accord.

Rather I think it has to do with desire -  the desire to take up the 
responsibility for the quality of one's life, to rely as much as 
possible on one's own efforts, to maintain exacting standards of 
independent functioning & hold oneself accountable for one's actions.  
Expressions I've heard before, like "intellectual integrity" and 
"allowing contact with one's critical faculties", come to mind.

Blanc



